<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tutuila</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tutuila is the largest and most populous island of American Samoa, in the South Pacific. Its deep harbour of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pago-pago">Pago Pago</a> attracted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, which secured rights to a naval coaling station there by treaty in 1878 and received the island by deed of cession from local chiefs in 1900. It holds the territory's seat of government and most of its population.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tutuila.jpg" alt="Tutuila" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA, Original uploader: GarciaB · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:TutuilaFromSpace.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tutuila is part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samoan-islands">Samoan Islands</a> and is the third largest island of that chain. It lies roughly 4,000 kilometres northeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brisbane">Brisbane</a> and over 1,200 kilometres northeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>. It is a volcanic island of early Pliocene age, about 33 kilometres long and little more than 4.8 kilometres across at its widest point. The highest point is Matafao Peak at 653 metres.</p><p>Pago Pago Harbor, a large natural harbour, divides the island into two parts. The island has six terrestrial and three marine ecosystems, with lowland and montane rain forest. The National Park of American Samoa, established in 1988, protects part of the forest and coast, including two species of flying fox that Samoans regard as guardians of the forest and its people.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesians">Polynesians</a> first reached Samoa around 1000 BCE, and by 600 BCE they had a settlement on Tutuila at Tula. Around 1200 CE, in the reign of Momo, the tenth Tuʻi Tonga, the Tongans invaded and occupied Tutuila and other Samoan islands for nearly two centuries. Under the paramount chief Malietoa the Samoans rose against them, and Fau'au of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pago-pago">Pago Pago</a> led the local resistance that expelled the Tongans from Tutuila.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacob-roggeveen">Jacob Roggeveen</a> encountered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samoan-islands">Samoan Islands</a> in 1722, and Louis Antoine de Bougainville named them the Navigator Islands in 1768. Whalers and Protestant missionaries arrived in the early 19th century, and traders developed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apia">Apia</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upolu">Upolu</a> rather than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pago-pago">Pago Pago</a> as their station.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-navy">US Navy</a> saw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pago-pago">Pago Pago</a> as an ideal refuelling station for the new San Francisco-to-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sydney-nova-scotia">Sydney</a> steamship service, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-congress">United States Congress</a> ratified a treaty for it in 1878. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> also had interests, and in 1879 the three powers formed a tripartite government. Their dispute led in 1899 to a division of the islands: the eastern islands, including Tutuila, became American Samoa and the western ones came under European control. The US flag was raised on Tutuila on 17 April 1900.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Tutuila holds about 95 per cent of the population of American Samoa and some 68 per cent of its land. The capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pago-pago">Pago Pago</a>, stands on the harbour, and Pago Pago International Airport is on the island. American Samoa is an unincorporated and unorganized territory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and more than 90 per cent of its land is communally owned. Relics of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, including guns and bunkers around the harbour, survive on the island.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tutuila” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Chiefs of <b>Tutuila</b> and Aunuʻu signed a deed of cession in 1900, and the rulers of the Kingdom of Manuʻa ceded their islands in 1904.</blockquote><a href="/state/american-samoa">American Samoa</a></li></ul></section>
